content:
{
  "hostname": "BL-LT-0106",
  "reason": "Check disk space for file save failures"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, sorry this has dragged on, and thanks for sticking with it. I found the problem: your laptop's drive is almost full. It has only about 9 GB free out of 256 GB (3.5%), and that's why files won't save. Here's how to free up space:\n1. Empty the Recycle Bin, and delete or move anything in your Downloads folder that you don't need.\n2. Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Select temporary files and old Windows Update files, then click Remove.\n3. Turn on OneDrive Files On-Demand. Right-click the OneDrive cloud icon near the clock, then go to Settings > Sync and back up > Advanced settings > Files On-Demand and choose to free up disk space. Your synced files stay in the cloud until you open them.\n4. Go to Settings > Apps and uninstall any apps you no longer use.\nWhen you're done, reply here and I'll check your free space from our side. If it's still too low, I'll send it straight to our senior team to look at a bigger drive or a replacement laptop.",
  "internal_note": "User says this is the third request and is frustrated. Device BL-LT-0106 (assigned to leo.martins, online, Win11 Pro) has 9.0 GB free of 256 GB (3.5%) and a low disk space warning. That matches 'can't save files'. Account is active with no lockout. Gave the user the KB-017 cleanup steps (Recycle Bin/Downloads, temp files and old update files, OneDrive Files On-Demand, uninstall unused apps). Next step: re-check device status after cleanup. If free space is still under 10% (about 25.6 GB), escalate to Tier 2 under KB-017 for a larger drive or replacement. Given the repeat request, consider handling quickly or escalating early. I couldn't find the earlier tickets.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-017"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
